  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Through FY2026 to date, federal contract obligations in Maryland total $23.7B. There are 66,723 awards, and the average award size is $355K. Obligations fell 40.3% year over year. These totals are fiscal year to date and therefore partial. The Department of Defense accounts for the largest share of buying at $9.4B, followed by the Department of Health and Human Services at $4.7B and the Department of Energy at $1.9B. THE JOHNS HOPKINS UNIVERSITY APPLIED PHYSICS LABORATORY LLC leads vendors with $1.3B, and ORANO FEDERAL SERVICES LLC and AMERICAN CENTRIFUGE OPERATING, LLC each have $900M. Major industry categories include Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences at $2.6B, Engineering Services at $2.5B, and Computer Systems Design Services at $2.5B. The data show concentration in defense, health, energy, and advanced research services. Keep in mind the year to date note: totals reflect partial fiscal-year obligations.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What changed year over year?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 40.3% year over year. Total obligations for FY2026 to date are $23.7B, down from prior full fiscal-year levels. Award activity still shows 66,723 awards and an average award size of $355K, which frames the reduced total as a function of both volume and timing. The FY series shows prior full-year obligations ranging from $39.2B to $46.3B."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Where is the money going in Maryland?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense leads buyers with $9.4B, followed by the Department of Health and Human Services at $4.7B and the Department of Energy at $1.9B. Vendors concentrated at the top include THE JOHNS HOPKINS UNIVERSITY APPLIED PHYSICS LABORATORY LLC at $1.3B, and two vendors at $900M each. Spending clusters in R&D ($2.6B), engineering services ($2.5B), and computer systems design ($2.5B)."
      }
    ]
